 ## Subtopics
 
-### 4D Extension
-This approach extends static 3D compression methods to 4D by treating the time dimension as a fourth spatial axis [S1]. It exploits voxel correlation across subsequent frames to achieve high compression ratios [S1]. However, it introduces dependencies between frames, which increases rendering overhead when accessing a single time step, and struggles with discrepancies between spatial and temporal resolutions [S1].
+### [DCT-compressed time-runs rendering](13569-dct-compressed-time-runs-rendering.md)
+This method enables interactive rendering of large-scale time-varying volumetric datasets by treating video memory, main memory, and disk as a three-level cache. It solves the bandwidth bottleneck of transferring thousands of time steps by compressing temporal sequences of voxel values into single scalar indices using the Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT). Unlike sibling methods that compress color or opacity data, this approach compresses the index data itself, allowing color and opacity transfer functions to be modified without recompression. It sets itself apart by leveraging hardware palette manipulation to decode time-varying data on the fly, avoiding the need for complex on-the-fly inverse transforms during rendering. See [DCT-compressed time-runs rendering](13569-dct-compressed-time-runs-rendering.md).
 
-### Key-Frame + Differential Encoding
-This strategy combines spatial compression of key frames with separate temporal compression, analogous to video encoding [S1]. It exploits temporal coherence via spatial subdivision and compact block encoding [S1]. This method offers better random access capabilities and good compression rates compared to full 4D extension, as it avoids deep frame dependencies for non-key frames [S1].
-
-### Per-Step Encoding
-This method encodes each time step separately using static compression techniques [S1]. It provides full temporal random access and independence between frames [S1]. The trade-off is reduced compression rates, as temporal correlations are not exploited [S1].
+### [Subsampled Reconstruction](14066-subsampled-reconstruction.md)
+Subsampled Reconstruction is a technique for time-varying volume rendering that enables fluent user interaction by decoding only a subsampled portion of the compressed data rather than the full time step. It addresses the latency bottleneck in real-time exploration of large 4D datasets, where full reconstruction times can exceed 100ms. Unlike full reconstruction or sibling methods like DCT-compressed time-runs rendering, it prioritizes interaction speed over spatial fidelity by skipping a significant fraction of the coefficient decoding. See [Subsampled Reconstruction](14066-subsampled-reconstruction.md).
 
 ### Temporal Encoding of Indexed Volumetric Data
-A method for temporal encoding of indexed volumetric data allowing quick hardware decoding, using OpenGL color palette manipulation to represent grid points at several times using a single scalar value [S2, unofficial]. The palette consists of limited sets of colors that sample a single value. The palette is varied to decode the approximate scalar value of a voxel for each frame properly, after which the scalar value is mapped to the desired opacity and color scalar index [S2, unofficial]. The method encodes a sequence of temporally changing scalar values inside a texel, where each possible index value represents a sample in the space of possible time-varying scalar values. Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) is used to map sequences of scalars into single scalar indices [S2, unofficial]. This allows representation of grid points at several times using a single scalar value, whereas standard methods might require storing full time-series data [S2, unofficial]. Tested on an NVIDIA GeForce2GTS graphics card with 64 megabytes of texture memory, achieving a compression ratio of 5:1 for volume data of $128^3$ [S2, unofficial]. Some blockiness and contouring were reported [S2, unofficial].
